Task 1:
Pasted Text of Results:
(.venv) melissa-ordonez@melissa-ordonez-VirtualBox:~/.openclaw/workspace/Documents/csce465-agentsec/hw2$ python3 baseline_ctr.py

Original Plaintext
{"action":"READ","path":"notes.txt"}

Original Text
7b22616374696f6e223a2252454144222c2270617468223a226e6f7465732e747874227d

Modified Text
7b22616374696f6e223a2253454e44222c2270617468223a226e6f7465732e747874227d

Original Ciphertext:
7847911069f6aa8a70ab4ec7645ae1f3317ff25c214aa19e350838c337dca4eddd55623a

Original XOR Modified
000000000000000000000001000f00000000000000000000000000000000000000000000

Modified Ciphertext:
7847911069f6aa8a70ab4ec66455e1f3317ff25c214aa19e350838c337dca4eddd55623a

Ciphertext XOR modified ciphertext:
000000000000000000000001000f00000000000000000000000000000000000000000000

Receiver First Attempt
Reciever decoded: {"action":"SEND","path":"notes.txt"}
Receiver Second Attempt
Reciever decoded: {"action":"SEND","path":"notes.txt"}

What the design is:
The code starts with creating the original message and then a modified version of that message where READ is replaced by SEND. A encryption key and another random value are generated and used to encrypt the original message. Then there exists a relay function which takes the original text, the modified text and the original ciphertext. This function gets the XOR of the original and modified texts (delta) to determine the bits that need to be flipped to get from the original to the modified. Then it returns the XOR of the ciphertext with delta which creates a modified ciphertext corresponding to the modified text. After this, the code calls a receiver function to decode the modified ciphertext. The same ciphertext gets sent to the receiver twice along with the original key and nonce value and that returns the decoded modified text.

What happened:
What happened when I ran this was that the relay function was able to change the message from READ to SEND without the key using only XOR. Additionally, the receiver function decoded the ciphertext twice.

What is the root cause:
The root cause of these results is that even without the key, the use of XOR makes changes in the ciphertext predictable if the attacker knows some or all of the original message. The cause for the receiver function decoding the ciphertext twice is that this form of encryption/decryption has no way of checking if a message is new or if it has been seen by the receiver before. So an attacker could continue to re-send the same message and the receiver wouldn't know.

Task 2:
Connect design to textbook concepts
Diffie-Hellman Key Establishment:
This design uses the textbook concept of Diffie-Hellman key establisdhment by getting the values of a large prime p and generator g from the ffdhe3072.pem file. Then using functions from the cryptography library, generates private and public keys for both Node and Gateway. The public keys are part of the handshake so that Node and Gateway know each others public keys. The shared secret key is then generated by both Gateway and Node using their own private key and the other's public key. 

Digital Signatures and Entity Authentication:
The code generates a an RSA private and public key for both Node and Gateway. After the handshake transcript is created and hashed, each party uses their private RSA key to sign the role and transcript hash. The handshake information and signature is then sent to each other, and they verify each other's signatures with the other party's public RSA key. This ties into the textbooks concepts of entity authentication because verifying the signatures makes the information came from the right person so the handshake is valid.

Nonces and Replay Resistance:
In the design, both Gateway and Node get a nonce which is a random 16 byte value. This value is included as part of the transcript in the handshake and changes every session. This ties into the textbooks concept of replay resistance because since new nonce values are generated each session, an old handshake can't be resent because the signature signs the role and transcript hash and the value of the transcript hash would change if the session has a new nonce.

Forward Secrecy:
This design has the textbook concept of forward secrecy because the Node and Gateway private keys are not reused and are generated fresh each session so there is a new shared key each session. Old session keys cannot be reconstructed.

Key Separation:
The design has the textbook concept of key separation by creating different keys based on the direction the message is traveling with different keys for gateway to node and node to gateway. There are also different keys for encryption and MAC. 

Signing both identities, roles, nonces and Diffie-Hellman values prevents basic man in the middle because if any of those values are changed by an attacker then the transcript hash would change and the public RSA key would not verify the signature on this changed message. It also prevents reflection attacks for similar reasons because an attacker would need to intercept the message from either Gateway or Node, change who it is from and send it back to the user that originally sent it. But in doing so this would change the transcript hash and the signature would not be accepted since it is not what was expected.

What is possible if signatures are removed:
If the signatures are removed, it could be possible for attackers to manipulate messages to and from Gateway or Node since they would not be able to authenticate that the message was written by the user it says it is from.

Task 4:

Security Note:
For this assignment we are assuming the attacker is someone trying to read or modify the encrypted messages between Node and Gateway or send malicious messages of their own. We assume the attacker would not have a way to physically take over the computer of either user. CTR requires a unique IV to be used for each encryption because the use of XOR leaves the encryption method vulnerable if an attacker knows the original message since they can XOR the original message with the ciphertext to get the encryption key. Different IVs makes sure that the encryption key that is XOR'd with the plaintext to get the ciphertext is unique each time so if an attacker discovers the key in one instance they cannot reuse it to read following messages. Verify-before-decrypt matters because it is important to make sure that the message that you are receiving is from the right person and has not been tampered with. It could be dangerous to open a message from an attacker since it could contain some kind of bug or malware so it is good practice to make sure the message is safe before decrypting it. Independent encryption is used to ensure that if an attacker cracks the secret key of some part of the communication in case of Task 2 say the key for gateway to node communication, then node to gateway communication is still safe since it uses a different key and is encrypted independently. So it is to add another layer of security to make sure if something is exposed, there are still other modes of encryption to keep the rest of the information secure. MAC keys are used to make sure that a message being received is from the right person and that it hasn't been tampered with before it was received. The exist to authenticate both sides of communication so both parties can know that the message they are receiving is safe. AES-GCM is a way to encrypt messages and to authenticate the message at the same time. As compared with AES-CTR which just allows for the encryption of messages and is more vulnerable to attacks unless a MAC is used as we proved in Task 1. A valid protected record makes sure that the message being sent is from the right person and is not tampered with but does not check what the message contains. If the message contained a dangerous tool call then that will still be accepted by the receiving party as long as it passes the authentication checks.

